0 2400w\" src=\"https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/project-suncatcher-google.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=9.5668629100084%2C0%2C80.866274179983%2C100&w=2400\"></p><p><img alt=\"project-suncatcher-google\" data-chromatic=\"ignore\" decoding=\"async\" data-nimg=\"fill\" sizes=\"(max-width: 768px) 100vw, 700px\" srcSet=\"https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/project-suncatcher-google.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=9.5668629100084%2C0%2C80.866274179983%2C100&w=376 376w, https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/project-suncatcher-google.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=9.5668629100084%2C0%2C80.866274179983%2C100&w=384 384w, https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/project-suncatcher-google.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=9.5668629100084%2C0%2C80.866274179983%2C100&w=415 415w, https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/project-suncatcher-google.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=9.5668629100084%2C0%2C80.866274179983%2C100&w=480 480w, https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/project-suncatcher-google.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=9.5668629100084%2C0%2C80.866274179983%2C100&w=540 540w, https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/project-suncatcher-google.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=9.5668629100084%2C0%2C80.866274179983%2C100&w=640 640w, https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/project-suncatcher-google.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=9.5668629100084%2C0%2C80.866274179983%2C100&w=750 750w, https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/project-suncatcher-google.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=9.5668629100084%2C0%2C80.866274179983%2C100&w=828 828w, https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/project-suncatcher-google.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=9.5668629100084%2C0%2C80.866274179983%2C100&w=1080 1080w, https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/project-suncatcher-google.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=9.5668629100084%2C0%2C80.866274179983%2C100&w=1200 1200w, https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/project-suncatcher-google.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=9.5668629100084%2C0%2C80.866274179983%2C100&w=1440 1440w, https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/project-suncatcher-google.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=9.5668629100084%2C0%2C80.866274179983%2C100&w=1920 1920w, https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/project-suncatcher-google.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=9.5668629100084%2C0%2C80.866274179983%2C100&w=2048 2048w, https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/project-suncatcher-google.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=9.5668629100084%2C0%2C80.866274179983%2C100&w=2400 2400w\" src=\"https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/10/project-suncatcher-google.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=9.5668629100084%2C0%2C80.866274179983%2C100&w=2400\"></p></div><p><cite>Image: Google</cite></p></div></div></div><div><div><div><p><a href=\"/authors/emma-roth\"><img alt=\"Emma Roth\" data-chromatic=\"ignore\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"36\" height=\"36\" decoding=\"async\" data-nimg=\"1\" srcSet=\"https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/chorus/author_profile_images/195810/EMMA_ROTH.0.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0%2C0%2C100%2C100&w=48 1x, https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/chorus/author_profile_images/195810/EMMA_ROTH.0.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0%2C0%2C100%2C100&w=96 2x\" src=\"https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/chorus/author_profile_images/195810/EMMA_ROTH.0.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0%2C0%2C100%2C100&w=96\"></a></p><div><p><span aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-haspopup=\"true\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\"><span id=\"follow-author-standard_article_details-dmcyOmF1dGhvclByb2ZpbGU6MTE2\"><span><span><svg width=\"9\" height=\"9\" viewBox=\"0 0 9 9\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" aria-label=\"Follow\"><path d=\"M5 0H4V4H0V5H4V9H5V5H9V4H5V0Z\" /></svg></span></span><span>Emma Roth</span></span></span></p> <p><span>is a news writer who covers the streaming wars, consumer tech, crypto, social media, and much more. Previously, she was a writer and editor at MUO.</span></p></div></div><div id=\"zephr-anchor\"><p>Google’s AI chip-equipped satellite has generated its first response from space. In a blog post on Friday, <a href=\"https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/google-research/project-suncatcher-satellite-response/\">Google says</a> it successfully booted up the satellite’s Tensor Processing Units (TPUs), allowing the on-board Gemma 4 model to follow through on a prompt to create a haiku about being in space:</p><div><blockquote><p>Blue marble below</p><p>Data streams in silent grace</p><p>Starlight feeds my core. ✨</p></blockquote></div><p>Google says it tried out other space-themed limericks and telemetry prompts as well. The test is part of Google’s Project Suncatcher, <a href=\"/tech/1000015/google-ai-satellite-space-project-suncatcher\">an experimental initiative</a> that could eventually put AI data centers in space. Google launched the satellite into orbit <a href=\"/tech/1003838/googles-ai-satellite-successfully-launches-into-space\">earlier this month</a>.</p><p>“Next, we will continue with a series of tests to evaluate how TPUs perform under thermal stress and radiation exposure in orbit,” Google says. “Verifying our TPUs can reliably execute workloads in space paves the way for our next milestone of twin interconnected satellites in 2027.”</p><div><p><span><strong>Follow topics and authors</strong> from this story to see more like this in your personalized homepage feed and to receive email updates.</span></p><ul><li id=\"follow-author-article_footer-dmcyOmF1dGhvclByb2ZpbGU6MTE2\"><span aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-haspopup=\"true\" role=\"button\" tabindex=\"0\"><span><span><svg width=\"9\" height=\"9\" viewBox=\"0 0 9 9\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" aria-label=\"Follow\"><path d=\"M5 0H4V4H0V5H4V9H5V5H9V4H5V0Z\" /></svg></span><span>Emma Roth</span></span></span></li><li></li><li></li><li></li><li></li><li></li><li></li></ul></div></div></div><!--$--><div><form><div><div><h2>The Verge Daily</h2><p><span>A free daily digest of the news that matters most.</span></p></div><div><fieldset><div><p><label for=\"email\">Email (required)</label></p></div></fieldset></div></div></form></div><!--/$--></div></article><!--$--><!--/$--></div></main></div></div></DIV>","content_text":"Skip to main contentThe Project Suncatcher satellite generated a haiku about being in space.The Project Suncatcher satellite generated a haiku about being in space.by Emma RothOct 30, 2026, 6:21 PM UTCImage: GoogleEmma Roth is a news writer who covers the streaming wars, consumer tech, crypto, social media, and much more. Previously, she was a writer and editor at MUO.Google’s AI chip-equipped satellite has generated its first response from space. In a blog post on Friday, Google says it successfully booted up the satellite’s Tensor Processing Units (TPUs), allowing the on-board Gemma 4 model to follow through on a prompt to create a haiku about being in space:Blue marble belowData streams in silent graceStarlight feeds my core. ✨Google says it tried out other space-themed limericks and telemetry prompts as well. The test is part of Google’s Project Suncatcher, an experimental initiative that could eventually put AI data centers in space. Google launched the satellite into orbit earlier this month.“Next, we will continue with a series of tests to evaluate how TPUs perform under thermal stress and radiation exposure in orbit,” Google says. “Verifying our TPUs can reliably execute workloads in space paves the way for our next milestone of twin interconnected satellites in 2027.”Follow topics and authors from this story to see more like this in your personalized homepage feed and to receive email updates.Emma RothThe Verge DailyA free daily digest of the news that matters most.Email (required)","fetched_at":"2026-11-02T10:11:57.898Z","ok":true}